    /**
     * Sustained automatic-fire loop for the local player.
     *
     * Retail holds one infinite loop while the trigger is down and plays a
     * separate tail when it closes. Firing a one-shot per bullet instead
     * machine-guns the sample, which is audibly wrong.
     *
     * The loop closes on the first QUANTISED boundary at which the trigger is no
     * longer live -- not on an idle timeout, which is what this replaces. The
     * distinction is audible rather than academic: these loop grains are exactly
     * one round long (the SMG's is 94 ms against a 100 ms cadence) and carry no
     * attack transient, so what the ear hears as a shot is the discontinuity at
     * the loop's wrap point. An idle timeout of 1.75 cadences left 73 ms of a
     * second iteration exposed and every tap sounded like two rounds; retail's
     * grid leaves about 6 ms, which the incoming tail masks.
     */
    struct FireLoopState final {
        std::uint8_t tool_id{};
        audio::LoopVoice voice{audio::invalid_loop_voice};
        world::FireLoopCadence cadence;
    };
